argument:[
{id:"S001",text:"First record two local facts. From D003 and D024, the squared source Sobolev norm is 2*pi times the squared target Sobolev norm at every real order. The identity on polynomials preserves Cauchy sequences and zero-distance equivalence when a norm is multiplied by the fixed positive scalar sqrt(2*pi); it therefore identifies their completions, with identical distributional Fourier coefficients. Consequently the carriers agree and ||h||_(Hsrc^s)=sqrt(2*pi)||h||_(H^s). At order zero, D004 and D025 give the same identity for L2src and L2. These identifications preserve zero mean. Also, whenever r<=s, each weight (1+k^2)^r is at most (1+k^2)^s; comparison of the nonnegative sums gives H^s contained in H^r with ||h||_(H^r)<=||h||_(H^s), and the analogous source inequality. These comparisons hold on the completions as well, by the identity extension. Next use only the epsilon quantifiers of E003 and E004 at sigma_s=1/2. Each phrase 'any fixed sufficiently small epsilon>0' supplies a positive upper threshold below which every positive epsilon is admissible. The intersection of these two intervals and (0,1/8] contains a positive number: half the minimum of their two positive thresholds and 1/8 is one such number. Hence F002 follows without claiming that 1/8 itself is admissible. This existence assertion involves only the fixed source statements at sigma_s=1/2, not any datum, grid or support.",
