{id:"F001",statement:"For every real s, Hsrc^s and H^s have the same periodic distributions and ||h||_(Hsrc^s)=sqrt(2*pi)||h||_(H^s); zero mean is preserved. At s=0 these are L2src and L2. For real r<=s, H^s is contained in H^r and ||h||_(H^r)<=||h||_(H^s), with the same assertion for source norms.",binders:[{symbol:"s",type:"real"},{symbol:"r",type:"real"},{symbol:"h",type:"periodic distribution"}],objects:["H","Hsrc","L2","L2src","s","r","h"],assumptions:[]},
{id:"F002",statement:"There exists a real eps with 0<eps<=1/8 admissible simultaneously for E003 and E004 at sigma_s=1/2: E003 holds with beta0=-1/2-eps for every beta in its displayed interval and all data and steps satisfying its other conditions, and E004 holds with a(1/2)=eps for all functions satisfying its other conditions. This choice can be made independently of all functions, grids, times and support.",binders:[{symbol:"eps",type:"real"}],objects:["eps"],assumptions:[]}
],introduces:[],discharges:[]},
{id:"S002",text:"Choose once eps as in F002; keep this fixed through the stage argument. Fix arbitrary periodic f,g and assume L1: f belongs to H^gamma and g belongs to H^agamma. H002 gives 0<gamma<=1. Apply E004 with sigma_s=gamma, source g=f and source h=g. If gamma<1/2 its required second exponent is 1/2-gamma=agamma, and if gamma>1/2 it is 0=agamma. If gamma=1/2, use epsilon=eps, which is admissible by F002. The required second exponent is then eps, rather than agamma=1/8. Since eps<=1/8, F001 gives g in H^eps and ||g||_(H^eps)<=||g||_(H^(1/8)). In all cases F001 supplies the actual source memberships and converts the source inequality, leaving a factor sqrt(2*pi) in its constant. Thus ||fg||_2<=C_gamma||f||_(H^gamma)||g||_(H^agamma). At gamma=1/2 the constant may depend on the single fixed eps chosen solely from the two source statements; it is independent of f,g and all numerical data. Discharge L1 and quantify f,g to obtain the unrestricted product assertion.",
uses:["H002","E004"],uses_local:["F001","F002"],objects:["eps","f","g","gamma","agamma","H","Hsrc","L2","L2src"],produces:[],introduces:["L1"],discharges:["L1"]},
{id:"S003",text:"Fix arbitrary real M0,tau,t, nonnegative integer n and periodic w. Assume L2: 0<tau<=1, t in [n*tau,(n+1)*tau], and w is time-independent, mean-zero, belongs to H^gamma and satisfies ||w||_(H^gamma)<=M0. In particular M0>=0. H002 and F001 give 0<gamma<=1 and w in Hsrc^gamma with ||w||_(Hsrc^gamma)<=sqrt(2*pi)*M0. In E003 set sigma_s=gamma, tau_s=tau, j=n, t_j=n*tau and g1=g2=w. For gamma=1/2 use epsilon=eps from F002, so beta0=-1/2-eps; otherwise use the source's prescribed beta0. The source integral is Stage_n(t;w): D005 identifies exp(s partial_x^3)=S_(-s) and exp(-s partial_x^3)=S_s, D016 identifies the integrand, and D021 has the same integral and factor 1/2. This equality holds on polynomials and for the distributional stage by extension; the source bounds provide its bounded extension in the norms used here. Apply E003 first with beta=agamma. For gamma<1/2, beta0=2*gamma-3/2 and agamma=1/2-gamma, giving agamma-beta0=2-3*gamma>0, agamma<=1+gamma, and theta=(1/2+2*gamma)/(5/2-gamma). Its denominator is positive, and theta>=gamma since the numerator minus gamma times the denominator is (gamma-1/4)^2+7/16>0. For gamma=1/2, beta0=-1/2-eps<agamma=1/8<3/2, and theta=(11/8)/(2+eps). Since 0<eps<=1/8, theta>=11/17>1/2=gamma. For gamma>1/2, beta0=gamma-1<=0=agamma<=1+gamma and theta=(1+gamma)/2>=gamma because gamma<=1. Thus each beta is allowed and each exponent denominator is positive. After F001 converts both sides, E003 yields ||Stage_n(t;w)||_(H^agamma)<=C_gamma*tau^theta*||w||_(H^gamma)^2. Because 0<tau<=1 and theta>=gamma, tau^theta<=tau^gamma: (theta-gamma)log(tau)<=0. This gives the first pointwise bound by C_gamma*M0^2*tau^gamma. Apply E003 again to the same data with beta=1+gamma; this upper endpoint is allowed in all three cases and its time exponent is zero. Norm conversion gives ||Stage_n(t;w)||_(H^(1+gamma))<=C_gamma*M0^2. Both constants are uniform in n,tau,t and support by E003; any dependence on eps is on the single fixed choice in S002. Taking one larger constant depending only on M0,gamma gives both asserted bounds. The stage has continuous representatives in these norms: the E003 endpoint formula, boundedness of Dinv from H^gamma to H^(1+gamma), strong continuity of the Airy multipliers, and continuity of multiplication on H^(1+gamma) give this. For completeness, the inverse-derivative bound follows from (1+k^2)/k^2<=2 on nonzero modes. Strong continuity follows by finite Fourier truncation and uniformly small square-summable tails. For r=1+gamma>1/2, multiplication is bounded on H^r by the weighted convolution inequality <k>^r<=C_r(<l>^r+<k-l>^r), the ell^1-ell^2 convolution bound, and Cauchy-Schwarz with sum_k<k>^(-2*r)<infinity. These establish the endpoint formula's continuity and the polynomial extension; H^(1+gamma) embeds in H^agamma by F001. The pointwise estimates with a common constant therefore bound both time suprema on the step as specified by D004. Discharge L2 and universally quantify the arbitrary stage data. Together with S002 this proves exactly N007; the auxiliary eps does not occur in its assertion.",
