const a=load("repaired_artifact");
a.inference.argument[0].text="D003 and D024 give ||h||_(Hsrc^s)^2=2*pi*||h||_(H^s)^2 at every real s. Multiplication of a norm by sqrt(2*pi)>0 preserves Cauchy sequences and zero-distance equivalence on polynomials. Thus the identity on polynomials identifies the completions as distributions with the same Fourier coefficients. It follows that ||h||_(Hsrc^s)=sqrt(2*pi)||h||_(H^s) and zero mean is preserved. D004 and D025 identify the order-zero spaces with L2 and L2src. For r<=s the weights satisfy (1+k^2)^r<=(1+k^2)^s, so comparison of the nonnegative sums yields H^s contained in H^r and ||h||_(H^r)<=||h||_(H^s); the source norms satisfy the identical comparison. This proves F001. To prove F002, interpret the sufficiently-small quantifiers in E003 and E004 at sigma_s=1/2: each guarantees a positive threshold such that every smaller positive epsilon is allowed in its estimate. Half the minimum of these thresholds and 1/8 is positive, is at most 1/8, and is below both thresholds. Therefore there exists a common admissible epsilon in (0,1/8]. Its existence and choice require only E003 and E004 with sigma_s=1/2, independently of functions, grids, times and support.";
store("repaired_artifact",a);text(await tools.check_draft({artifact:a}));

